      "text": "To determine the area of Mateen's rectangular backyard, we need to find the dimensions of the backyard based on the given information and then use those dimensions to calculate the area.\n\n1. **Determine the dimensions using the perimeter information:**\n   - Mateen walks the length 25 times to walk one kilometer.\n   - This means the perimeter of the backyard is \\( \\frac{1000 \\text{ m}}{25} = 40 \\text{ m} \\).\n\n2. **Determine the dimensions using the total distance information:**\n   - Mateen walks the perimeter 10 times to walk one kilometer.\n   - This means the perimeter of the backyard is \\( \\frac{1000 \\text{ m}}{10} = 100 \\text{ m} \\).\n\nSince both methods (number of walks) give different perimeters, we need to identify the correct method. The perimeter method is used here, so the perimeter is 40 meters.\n\nLet the length and width of the rectangular backyard be \\( l \\) and \\( w \\) meters, respectively. The perimeter of a rectangle is given by:\n\\[ 2l + 2w = 40 \\]\n\\[ l + w = 20 \\]\n\n3. **Determine which method is correct:**\n   - Using \\( l + w = 20 \\), we can substitute \\( w = 20 - l \\) into the area formula:\n   \\[ \\text{Area} = l \\cdot w = l \\cdot (20 - l) = 20l - l^2 \\]\n   - We can also assume the length and width are integers and the least common multiple of 25 and 10 is 50. Since the length and width were assumed to be integers summing to 20, suitable pairs are \\( l = 10 \\) and \\( w = 10 \\).\n\n4. **Calculate the area using the correct dimensions:**\n   - Given \\( w = 20 - l = 20 - 10 = 10 \\), we get:\n   \\[ \\text{Area} = l \\cdot w = 10 \\cdot 10 = 100 \\]\n\nThus, the area of the backyard is \\boxed{100} square meters.",
