      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to find the area of Mateen's rectangular backyard, given that he can either walk its length 25 times to cover a kilometer (1000 meters) or walk its perimeter 10 times to cover the same distance.\n\nWe have two pieces of information from the problem statement:\n1. Length * 25 = 1000 m\n2. Perimeter * 10 = 1000 m\n\nLet's solve for the length using the first piece of information:\n\\[ \\text{Length} \\times 25 = 1000 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Length} = \\frac{1000}{25} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Length} = 40 \\text{ m} \\]\n\nNow, we know the length of the backyard is 40 meters. Next, let's use the second piece of information to solve for the width.\n\\[ \\text{Perimeter} \\times 10 = 1000 \\]\n\\[ 2 \\times (\\text{Length} + \\text{Width}) \\times 10 = 1000 \\]\n\\[ 2 \\times (40 + \\text{Width}) \\times 10 = 1000 \\]\n\\[ 2 \\times 40 + 2 \\times \\text{Width} \\times 10 = 1000 \\]\n\\[ 80 + 20 \\times \\text{Width} = 1000 \\]\n\\[ 20 \\times \\text{Width} = 1000 - 80 \\]\n\\[ 20 \\times \\text{Width} = 920 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Width} = \\frac{920}{20} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Width} = 46 \\text{ m} \\]\n\nNow that we have the length and width of the backyard:\n- Length = 40 m\n- Width = 46 m\n\nTo find the area of the backyard, we multiply these two dimensions:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\text{Length} \\times \\text{Width} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Area} = 40 \\times 46 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Area} = 1840 \\text{ m}^2 \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of Mateen’s backyard is \\boxed{1840} square meters.",
